#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: v2-mirror-surface
description: the compiler-synthesized `Mirror` exposes the same members on every lane — in particular `isProduct`, which the native lane answered with `Stub`
backends: [int, js, jvm]
---

# `Mirror` must answer the same on every lane

`BUGS.md` `v2-mirror-isproduct-stub`. The native `Mirror` implemented exactly **three** members —
the VM had arms for `label`, `elemLabels` and `elemTypes` and nothing else, and both self-hosted
fronts emitted a matching three-field value. `m.isProduct` therefore fell through to the ambient
plugin path and evaluated to **`Stub`** — and the program kept going and exited **0**.

That is not an abstract gap: `std/agent.ssc:66` guards derivation with `if !m.isProduct then
throw …`, so `examples/rozum-agent-schema-derived.ssc` died on `if: condition not Bool:
Stub("Mirror.isProduct")`. A member that silently evaluates to a sentinel instead of failing is
worse than a missing one — the `if` above it decided on a non-boolean.

The three already-working members are pinned alongside `isProduct` on purpose: the fix registers
`isProduct` as a *tagged method*, which `methodDispatch1` consults **before** the VM's hardcoded
arms, so getting it wrong would shadow the three that were fine. If a regression ever did that,
this case shows it as three changed lines, not one.

`fromProduct` is here too, as of the follow-up that closed it. It is the one member that cannot be
a constant: it has to CONSTRUCT the type, so the Mirror carries the constructor as a fourth field
(pre-shaped to take the argument list) and the tagged method just applies it. Pinning a build here
is what proves that field survived — a Mirror that merely *reports* the right labels would still
pass every other line in this case.

```scalascript
case class P(x: Int, s: String)
case class Solo(only: Boolean)

val mp = summon[Mirror.Of[P]]
println(mp.label)
println(mp.elemLabels)
println(mp.elemTypes)
println(mp.isProduct)

// A second product, so the registration cannot be accidentally per-program-single-type.
val ms = summon[Mirror.Of[Solo]]
println(ms.label)
println(ms.elemLabels)
println(ms.isProduct)

// `isProduct` must be a real Bool, not a truthy sentinel: it has to drive an `if`.
if mp.isProduct then println("guard-taken") else println("guard-missed")

// `fromProduct` must build a real instance from the element list, for both arities.
println(mp.fromProduct(List(1, "hi")))
println(ms.fromProduct(List(true)))
```
